 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+The worker keeps two replacement queues over cached sheets: **A1in** and **Am**. Both queues track JPEG bytes only; all queue membership, ordering, and sizing metadata is private to the worker and invisible to the review API.
+
+- **A1in** holds sheets admitted since their last render and is FIFO. Every newly completed sheet enters A1in at its tail. A1in records no recency beyond insertion order.
+- **Am** holds sheets that have been requested at least twice and is maintained in least-recently-used order. A hit on a sheet in A1in promotes it to Am's front; a hit on a sheet in Am moves it to Am's front.
+- **Eviction** serves admission only. When admitting a completed sheet would push total cached bytes over 12 GiB, the worker evicts from A1in's head first; only when A1in is empty does it evict from Am's least-recently-used end. A sheet evicted from A1in without a second request never reaches Am.
+- A sheet re-requested after eviction re-renders and re-enters as fresh first-use at A1in's tail.
+
+A1in is bounded to 3 GiB (one quarter of the cache budget). When a new sheet would push A1in past that bound, A1in's head is evicted at admission time by the rule above. The bound exists so that one-off screening traffic cannot grow to consume the whole cache: long sequential screenings fill A1in and cycle within it, while repeatedly compared sheets stay resident in Am.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q with A1in (FIFO, first-use) and Am (LRU, reused) queues | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | Editors alternate long sequential screenings with repeated comparisons of a small set; 2Q keeps reused comparison sheets resident in Am while screening scans cycle through A1in. LRU evicts comparison sheets during screenings; aged frequency counters retain yesterday's favorites after a project switch and add counter and aging maintenance. The extra 2Q bookkeeping stays private to the worker, so the HTTP contract, render queue, and 12 GiB budget are unchanged. |
+| A1in byte bound | 3 GiB (25% of the 12 GiB budget), tunable before pilot | Equal split; entry-count bounds | A quarter of the budget absorbs a screening's one-off sheets while leaving three quarters to reused sheets. Byte-based rather than count-based because sheets vary in size and the budget is expressed in bytes. Starting value is subject to tuning against the observed screening mix. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+1. ✅ Eviction policy is 2Q — chosen over LRU and frequency-with-aging to protect reused comparison sheets during long sequential screenings.
+
+### Deferred
+1. Tune the A1in/Am byte split (3 GiB starting value) from the pilot's observed screening mix; adjust without changing the external interface.
